## 2026-08-17 11:35 — v0.8.0: fix the stranded-brewable carry regression 0.7.0 introduced
Diagnosis (run #9 vs #8, sampled field frames): 0.7.0 lifted brew success
49%->87% but declining to brew minority/singleton herbs left them HOARDED
(_should_sell kept all 'brew' items). avg brewables held 0.49->1.83, chars at
full carry 0%->14.5%, avg carry frac 0.124->0.367. Full chars then repeatedly
tried (and failed on stamina) to walk home -> move/not_enough_stamina rose
7.5%->17.7% of actions (action-error rate 0.144->0.27). This is the exact
"minority-essence stranding" watch-item logged at 0.7.0.
Fix: (1) SELL stranded brewables — any herb that can't currently form a
no-curdle batch is banked for gold, not hoarded (new _brew_keep_ids marks the
batchable ones; _should_sell sells the rest). (2) Learn undecoded herbs with
same-KIND batches (share an essence -> can't curdle) instead of mixing different
unknowns — which also removes 0.7.0's only curdle source (its 3 failures were
mixed-unknown learning batches).
Expected effect: avg brewables held + full-carry% back toward the 0.6.0 floor;
move/not_enough_stamina rate back down; brew success stays high (now ~100% on
attempted batches, since only no-curdle batches are attempted); deaths/1k should
improve as chars stop stalling at full carry.
Falsified if: full-carry% or the move-stamina error rate does NOT fall vs run #9.
Verify: pytest 101 green + both fixes mutation-checked + bounded replay clean.
Next after measuring: v0.9.0 taste-to-learn (turn stranded undecoded singletons
into essence discoveries via `taste` instead of just selling them), then M3a
forging (armor).

## 2026-08-17 11:42 — bugfix: superseded run windows left open (attribution integrity)
Found during the 0.8.0 redeploy: run #9 (0.7.0) stayed stopped_at=NULL after
#10 superseded it. Cause: end_run() only runs on GRACEFUL shutdown, but the
hot-redeploy hard-kills the outgoing runner, so it never closes its window ->
two windows read "open" and per-run metrics double-count. Fix: begin_run() now
defensively closes any dangling open window (UPDATE runs SET stopped_at WHERE
stopped_at IS NULL) before opening the new one, so there is always exactly one
open run regardless of how the prior runner died. Test + mutation-checked in
test_storage.py. Repaired run #9's window in the live DB (stopped_at = its last
frame time). NOT redeploying for this alone — run #10 (0.8.0) keeps accumulating
a clean measurement window; the fix takes effect on the next redeploy (it will
close #10 cleanly then, or on any hard kill).

## 2026-08-17 11:58 — frames retention by ARCHIVE-then-prune (operator directive), core built
Disk is the only scaling constraint: guild_log.db grows ~7 GiB/day (raw frames),
would fill the 40 GiB free in ~5-6 days and take the walk-away bot down. CPU/RAM
are idle (24 GiB free). Operator chose: don't downsample or delete — ARCHIVE old
frames off-box, then reclaim local space.
Built (destination-agnostic, tested): steemer/archive.py (export a closed,
aged-out run's frames to gzip-JSONL that reconstructs the rows byte-for-byte;
manifest table; verify-gated prune; WAL checkpoint+VACUUM) and steemer/shippers.py
(Shipper protocol + LocalDirShipper + two-oracle verify). Safety invariant: a
frame is pruned ONLY after its archive is shipped AND independently re-observed
on the remote with matching size/sha256 — else the DB stays full but intact and
we alert. Both guards mutation-checked (drop the prune guard -> test fails; make
verify always-true -> oracle self-tests fail). Only closed runs older than the
retention horizon are eligible; the open run and recent frames are never touched.
Pending operator credentials: the concrete off-box transport (S3-compatible vs
rsync/scp over SSH) implements the same 2-method Shipper; then the orchestrator
(export->ship->verify->prune->checkpoint) runs on a schedule for hands-off
retention. events/decisions/metrics stay local (cheap, mined signal).

## 2026-08-17 12:54 — retention live: NAS mount + daily archival cron (walk-away disk safety)
Mounted the NAS //<your-nas>/<your-share> at /mnt/nas over SMB2/3 (smbnetfs FUSE;
base mount_smbfs is SMB1-only and TrueNAS has SMB1 off). Permanent via rc.d
(ops/smbnetfs.rc) + kld_list=fusefs + smbnetfs_enable. Creds in /root/.smb (600),
never in repo (verified). Built + tested end-to-end against the real NAS the full
export->ship->verify(size+sha256)->prune->checkpoint chain (see ops/retention.md).
Scheduled: cron for cal, daily 04:17, uv run tools/archive_frames.py, 48h hot
window. checkpoint() no longer VACUUMs (VACUUM needs an exclusive lock the live
bot blocks; freed pages are reused so the file plateaus ~14 GiB steady). Not
